        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battles of Lexington and Concord</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">19 April 1775, from sunrise at Lexington until dusk at Charlestown</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Middlesex County in the Province of Massachusetts Bay, in the towns of Lexington, Concord, Lincoln, Menotomy (present-day Arlington) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, along a road of about 17 miles (27 km) from Cambridge to Concord, in the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">British Army regulars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, about 700 under Lieutenant Colonel Francis Smith with Major John Pitcairn, joined at Lexington by a brigade of about 1,000 under Brigadier General Earl Percy, about 1,700 in all; Massachusetts militia and minutemen, some 4,000 over the day, turning out piecemeal, with Brigadier General William Heath taking command of a phase of the fighting toward the day's end</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The British searched Concord and found few supplies, then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> under heavy fire with heavy losses and reached Charlestown at dusk. The militia then blockaded the land approaches, which began the siege of Boston.</span></div>
        </div>
