    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The British expedition of about 700 men set out to seize colonial military supplies at Concord, but Patriot leaders had been warned weeks earlier and had moved most of them, so few weapons were taken and the march set off the fighting it was meant to prevent.</li><li>Who fired the first shot at Lexington is still uncertain: witnesses on each side blamed the other, many did not know, and David Hackett Fischer wrote that a shot was fired by an unknown person after both commanders had ordered their men to hold fire.</li><li>On the road back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> the British were attacked all day by militia who often fired from cover and from long range, and about half the day's fatalities on each side occurred at Menotomy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The riders who warned the countryside set off a system of 'alarm and muster' that used bells, drums, alarm guns, bonfires and a trumpet, and people in towns 25 miles (40 km) from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> knew of the British movements while the troops were still unloading their boats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

