    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1768</span><span class="rf-text">The British Army occupies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1774</span><span class="rf-text">Parliament passes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a> and colonial leaders respond with the Suffolk Resolves and a Massachusetts Provincial Congress</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1775</span><span class="rf-text">Parliament declares Massachusetts to be in a state of rebellion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1775</span><span class="rf-text">About 700 regulars are ferried across to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> at night, and Paul Revere, William Dawes and Samuel Prescott ride to warn the countryside</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1775</span><span class="rf-text">Regulars and militia exchange fire at Lexington, where eight militiamen are killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1775</span><span class="rf-text">Militia force the British companies back across the Old North Bridge at Concord</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1775</span><span class="rf-text">The British retreat under fire and are reinforced by Percy's brigade at Lexington, then reach Charlestown at dusk</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1775</span><span class="rf-text">Militia from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> surround <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, beginning the siege</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battles-of-lexington-and-concord" data-v="e3297787d7" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The militia stand on Lexington Common</b> About 80 Lexington militiamen formed up on the Common in plain sight, but not across the road to Concord. Pitcairn's advance guard entered Lexington, with Smith's remaining companies further down the road.</li><li><b>The Regulars charge and the militia disperse</b> After shots were fired, of which the accounts disagree on the first, the Regulars charged with bayonets. Eight Lexington men were killed and ten wounded, and the militia fell back.</li><li><b>The Regulars search Concord</b> Smith reformed the column and marched to Concord, where he divided it to search for supplies. Four companies under Captain Parsons went beyond the North Bridge, while Laurie's companies guarded it. Barrett's militia watched from a hill to the north.</li><li><b>The fight at the North Bridge</b> Barrett's militia advanced on the bridge and exchanged fire with Laurie's companies, who broke and fled to the grenadiers coming from the town. This isolated the four companies at the farm.</li><li><b>The column leaves Concord and is hit on the road</b> Parsons' companies returned to the town and the Regulars marched east. Militia converged on the road and the British charged up Brooks Hill, but the colonists held it and inflicted heavy casualties on the attackers.</li><li><b>Percy's brigade rescues the column at Lexington</b> Near the Lexington line Parker's men, some bandaged, ambushed the column from a hill, and Smith was wounded. Percy's brigade arrived with artillery and fired at extreme range, dispersing the pursuing militia.</li><li><b>Percy's column fights its way to Charlestown</b> The combined column set out with flankers on both sides and the rearguard rotated. Militia fought it all along the road, notably at Jason Russell's farm in Menotomy. Percy turned onto a track to Charlestown, and the circle of fire was broken.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-gage"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-gage.jpg" alt="Thomas Gage" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-gage">Thomas Gage</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Gage_John_Singleton_Copley.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Singleton Copley, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The British Army occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1768. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> of 1773, Parliament passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a> of 1774, which historians later renamed the Intolerable Acts. In the summer of 1774 colonial leaders in Suffolk County adopted the Suffolk Resolves, which declared the acts unconstitutional, recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and urged Massachusetts residents to form their own government and fight in its defence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> endorsed the Resolves on 17 September 1774, and the colonists formed the Massachusetts Provincial Congress. In February 1775 both houses of Parliament declared that a state of rebellion existed in the province.</p><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-gage">Thomas Gage</a>, military governor of Massachusetts, commanded roughly 3,000 British troops in Boston and had lost control of the colony outside the town. He planned to avoid open conflict by taking military supplies from the militias through small, secret and rapid strikes. In one such strike the British seized supplies, but the Patriots hid the stores from other searches in nearly bloodless events known as the Powder Alarms. On 14 April 1775 Gage received instructions from the Earl of Dartmouth to disarm the rebels and imprison their leaders. He decided against written orders for the arrest of leaders, fearing that might spark an uprising. The militias had gathered weapons, powder and supplies at Concord and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, and Patriot leaders learned weeks before the expedition that the stores might be at risk and moved most of them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>About 700 regulars, drawn from 11 of Gage's 13 infantry regiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, were put under the overall command of Lieutenant Colonel Francis Smith. Major John Pitcairn of the Marines commanded ten light infantry companies and Lieutenant Colonel Benjamin Bernard 11 grenadier companies. The force included 350 grenadiers and about 320 light infantry. Most of the captains were volunteers from all the regiments in Boston, attached to the companies at random, and the lack of familiarity between commanders and companies would cause communication and disciplinary problems. A second force, the 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> under Brigadier General Hugh Percy, about 1,000 strong with two 6-pounder guns, would follow later in the morning.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> law every town had to form a militia company of all males of 16 and over, properly armed, and the companies elected their own officers. The total colonial force over the day included some 4,000 militiamen from local militia and minuteman companies, from thirty towns. The Provincial Congress had organised the companies into regiments and brigades, but units turned out piecemeal and several provincial generals were not in a position to assert overall command. At Lexington about 80 men of the town's training band, which was not a minuteman company, stood under Captain John Parker, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regulars crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> on the night of 18 April and began their 17-mile (27 km) march at about 2 am. At sunrise on 19 April, as Pitcairn's advance guard entered Lexington, Parker's men stood in ranks on the Common, not blocking the road. Parker ordered them to disperse and not fire, but a shot was fired and the regulars charged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonets</a>. Eight militiamen were killed and ten wounded, while one British soldier was wounded. Order was restored by Smith and the column marched on to Concord.</p><p>At Concord Smith split his force to search for supplies. About 100 light infantry in seven companies held the North Bridge, and four companies went on to Barrett's Farm. At about 11:00 am at least 400 militiamen advanced on the 90 to 95 men guarding the bridge. A shot rang out, the British fired a ragged volley, and two Acton minutemen were killed. Major Buttrick then ordered the militia to fire, and the British fled. The regulars left Concord after noon.</p><p>On the march back more militia arrived and fired from cover, notably at Meriam's Corner, Brooks Hill and the Bloody Angle. Smith was wounded in the thigh and Pitcairn thrown from his horse. At about 2:30 pm Percy's brigade met the column at Lexington, and the combined force of about 1,700 set out again at 3:30 pm. The fighting was heaviest at Menotomy and Cambridge. Percy turned off towards Charlestown and the British reached it near dark.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At Meriam's Corner two regulars were killed and perhaps six wounded, with no colonial casualties. At two sharp bends further on the British lost thirty killed or wounded and four militiamen were killed, three of them officers. At the North Bridge at least three privates of the 4th were killed or mortally wounded and nine more wounded. At the Jason Russell farm in Menotomy twenty-one colonial combatants and an unknown number of regulars died. At Menotomy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> the colonists lost 25 killed and nine wounded and the British 40 killed and 80 wounded, about half the day's fatalities for each side.</p><p>Exhausted and enraged troops were hard to control in the houses along the road. A few regulars committed atrocities, such as killing two drunk men at Cooper's Tavern. Taverns were ransacked and houses plundered, though colonists later exaggerated accounts of ransacking and burning, in part to gain compensation.</p><p>By the next morning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> was surrounded by a militia army of over 15,000 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, forming a siege line from Chelsea to Roxbury. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> adopted these men into the beginnings of the Continental Army. Gage refused to impose martial law and persuaded the selectmen to surrender private weapons in return for permission for inhabitants to leave. The Provincial Congress sent sworn depositions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where newspapers printed them two weeks before Gage's report arrived. In London, politicians tended to blame Gage rather than their own policies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The accounts of the first shot at Lexington conflict. Lieutenant John Barker of the 4th Foot wrote that the militia fired two shots at his regiment. Captain Parker testified in a sworn deposition that the regulars rushed the militia and fired without provocation, and 34 militiamen deposed that nobody in their company fired before they were fired on. The Massachusetts Provincial Congress collected scores of sworn testimonies from militiamen and British prisoners within four days of the battle, and unfavourable depositions were not published and were returned to the participants, Paul Revere among them. Isaiah Thomas recalled Parker's words many years later, and older participants in later life all said the British fired first, though many had said at the time that they were unsure.</p><p>Modern historians differ in detail. Fischer wrote that both Pitcairn and Parker ordered their men to hold fire and that a shot came from an unknown person, possibly several near-simultaneous shots. Mark Urban wrote that one or two villagers opened fire and a British company then fired a volley without orders from Pitcairn. At the North Bridge, Captain Walter Laurie's later report called the first shot a warning shot by a panicked British soldier. Whether Margaret Gage was the source of the colonists' advance knowledge of the date is described only as reasonable speculation. Colonel Timothy Pickering was accused of letting the British pass at Winter Hill to avoid war, but he said he acted on Heath's orders, which Heath denied.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-adams"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-adams.jpg" alt="John Adams" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart,_John_Adams,_c._1800-1815,_NGA_42933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gilbert Stuart, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The expedition was a failure, since the battles caused the fighting it was meant to prevent and few weapons were seized. Parliament's George Germain, no friend of the colonists, wrote that the Bostonians were right to make the King's troops the aggressors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, who rode along the battlefields the next day, became convinced that the die was cast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s 'hardened, sullen-tempered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>' after the news, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> wrote that America would be either drenched in blood or inhabited by slaves.</p><p>It mattered to the early American government to maintain an image of British fault and American innocence, and paintings showed the Lexington fight as an unjustified slaughter. Early in the nineteenth century the dispute over the first shot grew and the battle took on an almost mythical quality, so that later paintings showed the militia standing and fighting back. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a>'s 'Concord Hymn' of 1837 helped Americans learn about the Revolution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Longfellow</a>'s 'Paul Revere's Ride' was memorised by generations of schoolchildren after 1860, though it is historically inaccurate and Revere never reached Concord.</p><p>The Lexington Battle Green is a National Historic Landmark, and Minute Man National Historical Park holds the North Bridge and about 5 miles (8 km) of the road. Patriots' Day is a legal holiday in Massachusetts, observed on the third Monday in April, and on 19 April 1875 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> joined 50,000 people at the centenary.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 19 April 1775 about 700 British regulars sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> to seize supplies at Concord fought Massachusetts militia at Lexington, at Concord's North Bridge and along the road back. The militia then besieged Boston, and the fighting is counted the first major action of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>.</em></p>

